================================================================================
APPENDIX: STANDARD PHASES AND ROUTES
================================================================================

For full projects whose governing document names no phases. Trim to what the
project involves; skipped phases stay as ⏭ rows with the route as the reason.

The spine that is never skipped, however small the job: 1 (the ask written down)
→ 2 (signed, and any deposit cleared, before build) → 3 (sources of truth and the
AI-vendor gate) → 10 (security, scaled) → 11 (never straight to production) → 13
(handover tied to the final payment trigger).

   1  Discovery and intake: the real problem, in the client's words.
   2  Proposal, contract, onboarding: quote, sign, deposit, kickoff; terms set.
   3  Sources of truth: everything real consolidated into docs/sot.md.
   4  IA and UX audit (redesigns): what exists, what's broken, the URL inventory.
   5  Design system: direction, tokens, type, spacing.
   6  Mockups and review: sign-off before it gets expensive.
   7  Build setup: specs, plans, the codebase AGENTS.md.
   8  Implementation: risk-first, split into sub-phases, checkpoint demos.
   9  QA and testing: devices, accessibility, performance, content.
  10  Security and hardening: scaled to the attack surface; never skipped.
  11  Deployment: preview or staging before production.
  12  SEO and analytics: set up and verified; preserved on a redesign.
  13  Handover: docs, credentials, ownership, final payment.
  14  Maintenance: terms decided, not defaulted.
  15  Retrospective: lessons go back into the starter.

Routes (a project can be a blend; record each part):

  A  Greenfield (new site or app, no design yet): skip 4.
  B  Redesign of an existing site: all phases. 4 also produces content-migration
     decisions and the URL inventory; 11 runs a cutover (redirects live before
     DNS moves, email untouched); 12 preserves existing SEO. Get the existing code
     running instead of scaffolding.
  C  Design provided (Figma or brand book): skip 4. 5 extracts tokens from the
     design and lists what it doesn't specify (states, breakpoints, validation);
     6 shrinks to a fidelity check plus sign-off on the gap-filling decisions.
  D  Web app (auth, data, dashboards): 4-6 shrink to key screens; 7-8 grow, with
     data model, auth, and API contract as sub-phases; 10 runs in full; 12 swaps
     SEO for product analytics.
  E  Maintenance or small fixes: 1-3 collapse into one intake; no mockups or
     design system; work session by session; 10 still applies, scaled.
  F  Backend or API only: skip 4-6; API-contract design replaces 5 inside 7; the
     SEO half of 12 usually doesn't apply.
